    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1">

                <pb facs="0011" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg1"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/1–349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-01-03T15:00:00+08:00">January 3, 1949—3
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:39 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5. Although press is playing up Generalissimo’s<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1-f1"/> New
                    Year’s statement<anchor n="3" xml:id="d1-f2"/> as indication his willingness
                    step aside to permit peace, Lu Han, Governor Yunnan, who attended military
                    discussions informs us intention Generalissimo’s statement is “if Communists
                    want peace they can have peace; if they want war they can have war”.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1-f1" n="2" xml:id="d1-f1-content">
                <p>Chiang Kai-shek, President of the Republic of China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f2" n="3" xml:id="d1-f2-content">
                <p>Department of State, <hi rendition="#italic">United States Relations With
                        China</hi> (Washington, Government Printing Office, 1949), p. 920.</p>
            </note>
